00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:08.100
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.030
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.980
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.680
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.130
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.139 --> 00:00:19.129
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.010
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.389 --> 00:00:24.579
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.250
time for the final part of our

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:30.849
three-part series on famous con artists.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.060
In case you missed them, in part

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.759
one, we met Victor Lustig, the

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.609
man who sold the Eiffel Tower.

00:00:37.919 --> 00:00:39.670
In part two, it was Gregor

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.199
MacGregor, the Scottish soldier

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.330
who invented an entire country.

00:00:44.599 --> 00:00:47.399
But today it's a man whose name is

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.049
now a byword for fraud, Carlo Ponzi.

00:00:51.389 --> 00:00:53.509
So, let's not waste a minute

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:55.769
and get right into it.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.399
If you open up a dictionary and look up

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:03.219
the words "Ponzi scheme", you'll see a

00:01:03.219 --> 00:01:05.439
definition that's something like this:

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:09.530
a form of fraud in which early investors

00:01:09.739 --> 00:01:12.579
are paid with money taken from later

00:01:12.589 --> 00:01:15.419
investors, rather than any real profit.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.359
The term appears in newspapers

00:01:18.709 --> 00:01:20.029
practically every week.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.250
It has been applied to collapsed banks,

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.129
to cryptocurrency projects, to celebrity

00:01:26.139 --> 00:01:28.690
investment funds, and, in the biggest

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.349
case of all, to Bernie Madoff, who

00:01:31.489 --> 00:01:35.089
you might remember from episode 167.

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:38.799
Behind the dictionary entry there was

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:41.699
a real man, a small, smartly dressed

00:01:41.709 --> 00:01:44.649
Italian immigrant with a straw hat, a

00:01:44.669 --> 00:01:48.169
gold-topped cane and a dazzling smile.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.350
For eight glorious months in 1920,

00:01:52.699 --> 00:01:55.369
he was the most famous man in Boston.

00:01:55.889 --> 00:01:58.049
Crowds cheered him in the street.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.969
At the peak, a million dollars

00:02:00.989 --> 00:02:03.729
a day poured into his office.

00:02:04.529 --> 00:02:08.429
Of course, it was one great big lie;

00:02:08.659 --> 00:02:11.640
this was the original ponzi scheme.

00:02:12.939 --> 00:02:15.209
The man behind it, Carlo Ponzi,

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.329
was born in 1882 in a small town

00:02:19.559 --> 00:02:22.049
close to Bologna, in northern Italy.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.079
He didn't come from a particularly

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:28.200
wealthy family, and his parents scraped

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.769
together what money they could to send

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.730
him to university in Rome, hoping he would

00:02:33.759 --> 00:02:36.599
restore the family's previous fortunes.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:38.230
He did not.

00:02:39.009 --> 00:02:40.950
By his own account, he treated

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.229
university as a paid holiday,

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.139
spending four years in cafés and

00:02:46.149 --> 00:02:48.719
theatres until the money dried up.

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:51.319
He left with no degree,

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:54.330
no money and no prospects.

00:02:54.889 --> 00:02:57.569
So, like millions of other young

00:02:57.569 --> 00:02:59.769
Italians of his generation, he

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:01.879
got on a boat to America, the

00:03:01.879 --> 00:03:04.520
land where anything was possible.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.090
He arrived in Boston in

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.710
November 1903, aged twenty-one.

00:03:12.679 --> 00:03:15.520
He arrived with practically nothing — just

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.330
two dollars and fifty cents in his pocket.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.400
The rest of his money, he

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.439
would later say, he lost at the

00:03:22.469 --> 00:03:24.709
card tables during the voyage.

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:28.839
Like most Italian immigrants, his

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:31.219
early years in America were tough.

00:03:31.869 --> 00:03:34.699
He washed dishes and slept on the floor

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:36.849
of a restaurant kitchen, while trying

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:38.340
to learn English at the same time.

00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:41.339
He was promoted to waiter, and then

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.500
fired for shortchanging the customers,

00:03:43.869 --> 00:03:45.959
in other words, giving them less money

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.019
back in change than he should have.

00:03:49.139 --> 00:03:52.200
In 1907 he moved to Montreal, in

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.229
Canada, and got a job as a clerk

00:03:55.449 --> 00:03:57.270
in a bank called Banco Zarossi.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:03:59.839
It was a bank that served the city's

00:03:59.869 --> 00:04:02.529
Italian immigrants, and it was here

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.479
that he would learn an important lesson.

00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:10.250
Banco Zarossi paid six per cent

00:04:10.279 --> 00:04:13.059
interest on deposits, double

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:14.979
what anyone else was offering.

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:17.780
Of course, people queued up

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:19.459
to hand over their savings.

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:22.779
How did the bank manage to offer

00:04:22.779 --> 00:04:24.389
such generous interest rates?

00:04:24.959 --> 00:04:27.269
Well, the secret was that the

00:04:27.269 --> 00:04:29.299
generous interest payments were

00:04:29.299 --> 00:04:31.227
not coming from profits at all.

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:34.339
The bank was paying its older

00:04:34.339 --> 00:04:36.029
customers with the money

00:04:36.299 --> 00:04:38.329
arriving from the newer ones.

00:04:38.879 --> 00:04:41.009
When the gap grew too big to hide,

00:04:41.389 --> 00:04:43.210
the owner of the bank stuffed a

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:46.059
suitcase with cash and fled to Mexico,

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:48.589
leaving his customers with nothing.

00:04:49.329 --> 00:04:50.969
And, of course, leaving the young

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.379
Carlo, now Charles Ponzi, without a job.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.859
Ponzi found a chequebook belonging to

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.219
one of the bank's former customers, and

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:03.809
had the bright idea of writing himself a

00:05:03.809 --> 00:05:06.299
cheque from it, making it out to himself

00:05:06.619 --> 00:05:08.419
and forging the customer's signature.

00:05:09.329 --> 00:05:11.809
He was caught almost immediately, and

00:05:11.809 --> 00:05:14.299
spent three years in a Canadian prison.

00:05:15.419 --> 00:05:18.699
After he was released in 1911, he

00:05:18.709 --> 00:05:20.769
drifted back towards the United States

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:23.579
and promptly earned another two years in

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.629
prison, this time for helping to smuggle

00:05:26.649 --> 00:05:28.389
Italian immigrants across the border.

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:32.239
So, by his mid-thirties, Charles

00:05:32.239 --> 00:05:34.799
Ponzi's record read: no degree,

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.580
no money, two prison sentences,

00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:41.119
and a long list of failed schemes.

00:05:42.029 --> 00:05:46.879
But in 1918 his luck appeared to turn.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.229
Back in Boston, he met Rose Gnecco,

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:53.980
who was a quiet, kind young woman

00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:56.229
from an Italian-American family.

00:05:57.019 --> 00:05:59.269
They married, and Ponzi threw

00:05:59.269 --> 00:06:01.239
himself into one business idea

00:06:01.269 --> 00:06:03.640
after another, determined to get

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.960
his own slice of the American dream.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.340
One of those ideas involved publishing

00:06:10.349 --> 00:06:12.969
a trade magazine for exporters.

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:14.900
It failed, of course.

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:18.895
But because of it, in the summer of 1919,

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:22.849
a letter arrived from a company in Spain.

00:06:23.809 --> 00:06:27.310
And folded inside that letter was a small

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.680
piece of paper that would change his life.

00:06:30.619 --> 00:06:32.899
That piece of paper was something

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.050
called an international reply coupon.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.400
It solved a small, practical problem

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.969
of the postal age: how do you send

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:44.879
someone in another country the

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:47.059
postage for their reply, so they

00:06:47.059 --> 00:06:48.860
don't need to pay to reply to you?

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:51.520
You attach one of these

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:52.900
coupons with your letter.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.820
In 1906, the world's postal

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.229
services had agreed on this system.

00:06:59.869 --> 00:07:02.380
You could buy one of these coupons in

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.999
any member country, Spain, in this case.

00:07:05.439 --> 00:07:07.519
Send it abroad, and the person

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:10.570
receiving it could exchange it at

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:13.069
their local post office for stamps.

00:07:14.589 --> 00:07:15.849
The price of the coupon

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:18.219
was fixed in each country.

00:07:18.619 --> 00:07:20.639
And that, Ponzi realised,

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:22.749
was a crack in the system.

00:07:24.089 --> 00:07:26.700
After the First World War, most

00:07:26.709 --> 00:07:29.170
major European economies were on

00:07:29.189 --> 00:07:31.979
their knees, and currencies like

00:07:31.979 --> 00:07:34.629
the Italian lira had lost much of

00:07:34.629 --> 00:07:36.276
their value against the dollar.

00:07:36.849 --> 00:07:39.429
But the official coupon

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:41.789
prices had not been changed.

00:07:42.629 --> 00:07:44.679
Which meant you could take one

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.309
American dollar, change it into

00:07:47.329 --> 00:07:49.999
Italian lire, and buy a whole

00:07:50.029 --> 00:07:52.859
stack of reply coupons in Italy.

00:07:53.349 --> 00:07:56.209
Then ship them to the United States,

00:07:56.269 --> 00:07:58.949
exchange them for American stamps,

00:07:59.169 --> 00:08:01.450
and you would hold stamps worth

00:08:01.621 --> 00:08:03.679
far more than your original dollar.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:07.679
On paper, in theory, the profit

00:08:07.709 --> 00:08:10.079
was spectacular, potentially

00:08:10.099 --> 00:08:11.600
several hundred per cent.

00:08:12.669 --> 00:08:15.459
And, best of all, it was legal.

00:08:16.199 --> 00:08:18.779
It was a classic arbitrage strategy

00:08:19.119 --> 00:08:21.870
— buying something cheaply in one market and

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.259
selling it at a higher price in another.

00:08:25.099 --> 00:08:27.549
But if you're thinking, "hang on,

00:08:27.869 --> 00:08:30.079
it sounds fine in theory, but it's

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.369
a huge hassle to take stamps across

00:08:32.369 --> 00:08:35.239
the Atlantic and swap them for other

00:08:35.259 --> 00:08:36.869
stamps at post offices and then

00:08:36.869 --> 00:08:39.400
convert these for cash", you're right.

00:08:39.739 --> 00:08:41.709
But hold that thought.

00:08:42.369 --> 00:08:44.700
The important thing was the theory,

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.649
not the practicality, this was what

00:08:47.649 --> 00:08:49.759
Ponzi's entire business plan rested on.

00:08:51.069 --> 00:08:55.239
In December 1919, Ponzi founded a

00:08:55.239 --> 00:08:58.009
company with a grand, official-sounding

00:08:58.009 --> 00:09:00.910
name: the Securities Exchange Company.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:03.850
His offer was simple.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.610
Lend me your money, he said, and the

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:10.869
company would put it to work in the reply

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.750
coupon trade: buying coupons cheaply in

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:16.599
Europe, turning them into American stamps,

00:09:16.859 --> 00:09:19.250
and turning the stamps into dollars.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:22.709
The profits, he promised, were so

00:09:22.889 --> 00:09:26.069
enormous that he could pay you fifty

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:29.109
per cent interest in forty-five days.

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:31.240
In ninety days, he

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:32.719
would double your money.

00:09:34.559 --> 00:09:36.749
The first group of investors put

00:09:36.779 --> 00:09:39.229
in 1,800 dollars between them.

00:09:40.049 --> 00:09:42.629
Forty-five days later, Ponzi paid

00:09:42.629 --> 00:09:44.840
them their interest, in full.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:48.970
As word spread, more and more

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.359
investors came to Ponzi, practically

00:09:51.449 --> 00:09:53.100
begging him to take their money.

00:09:53.639 --> 00:09:55.780
The scheme really did work.

00:09:56.299 --> 00:09:58.750
After forty-five days, Ponzi's company

00:09:58.869 --> 00:10:01.209
would pay out fifty per cent interest.

00:10:01.629 --> 00:10:03.459
Wait another forty-five days

00:10:03.959 --> 00:10:05.449
and you would double your money.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.590
This little Italian man

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.049
could do the impossible.

00:10:11.979 --> 00:10:14.479
And the money started to flood in.

00:10:15.049 --> 00:10:18.388
A few thousand dollars in February 1920.

00:10:18.707 --> 00:10:20.230
Tens of thousands by March.

00:10:20.409 --> 00:10:22.659
By May, hundreds of thousands.

00:10:22.979 --> 00:10:26.140
By July, it was pouring in at a rate

00:10:26.304 --> 00:10:28.869
of a million dollars a day, with

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.069
queues stretching from his office

00:10:31.069 --> 00:10:33.539
on School Street around the block.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:37.639
Ponzi hired agents all over New England

00:10:37.959 --> 00:10:40.269
and paid them a commission of ten per

00:10:40.269 --> 00:10:43.929
cent, so an army of enthusiastic salesmen

00:10:44.299 --> 00:10:46.489
was soon recruiting on his behalf.

00:10:47.139 --> 00:10:49.449
Three quarters of the Boston police

00:10:49.449 --> 00:10:51.299
force was said to have invested.

00:10:51.629 --> 00:10:52.719
Priests invested.

00:10:52.769 --> 00:10:53.829
Widows invested.

00:10:54.049 --> 00:10:55.319
People mortgaged their

00:10:55.319 --> 00:10:57.790
houses to buy Ponzi's notes.

00:10:58.719 --> 00:11:01.350
Importantly, most people did

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.389
not even collect their gains.

00:11:04.049 --> 00:11:05.969
When their forty-five days were up,

00:11:06.369 --> 00:11:08.530
they simply reinvested everything,

00:11:08.759 --> 00:11:11.469
the original sum plus interest,

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.390
chasing the next fifty per cent.

00:11:14.339 --> 00:11:16.339
After all, why would you take

00:11:16.339 --> 00:11:18.359
your money out when Ponzi simply

00:11:18.389 --> 00:11:20.250
doubled it every ninety days?

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.489
There was no other investment

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:23.650
opportunity like it.

00:11:24.839 --> 00:11:26.859
The scheme, as you might imagine,

00:11:27.239 --> 00:11:29.110
made Ponzi a very rich man.

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:32.069
He bought a twelve-room mansion.

00:11:32.379 --> 00:11:33.369
He bought a Locomobile,

00:11:33.879 --> 00:11:35.300
the finest car in America.

00:11:35.649 --> 00:11:37.669
He bought a controlling stake in the

00:11:37.669 --> 00:11:41.370
Hanover Trust Company, a real Boston bank.

00:11:41.639 --> 00:11:43.249
He brought his elderly mother

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.950
over from Italy in a first-class

00:11:45.979 --> 00:11:47.710
cabin on an ocean liner.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:49.689
When he walked from his car to

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:51.969
his office, crowds cheered him.

00:11:52.889 --> 00:11:54.540
There's one story of someone

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:56.189
shouting to him that he was the

00:11:56.199 --> 00:11:58.690
greatest Italian who had ever lived.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:01.699
No, Ponzi protested: Columbus

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.189
had discovered America, and

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.590
Marconi had invented the radio.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:07.820
"But Charlie," came the

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.410
reply, "you discovered money!"

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.790
Now, you probably already know how

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:16.590
he was able to deliver such high

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:19.830
returns, and if you don't, you heard

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.550
it at the start of the episode with

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:23.690
the definition of a Ponzi scheme.

00:12:24.849 --> 00:12:27.320
The returns were not coming from

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:28.959
international reply coupons.

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:32.010
In fact, there is no evidence that Ponzi

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:34.159
ever really did any of this arbitrage.

00:12:34.939 --> 00:12:37.449
The money he paid out simply came

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:40.090
from people who invested afterwards.

00:12:40.779 --> 00:12:43.390
Like a pyramid, money was taken

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.349
from the bottom to pay those at

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.569
the top, people who got in earlier.

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:51.229
The mathematics of such a scheme never

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:53.389
work out in the end, because you run

00:12:53.389 --> 00:12:55.360
out of potential investors before long.

00:12:55.949 --> 00:12:58.859
But strangely enough, it seems

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:01.140
like Ponzi thought he could make it

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.739
work, that he could fill the hole by

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.650
investing in regular, honest businesses.

00:13:07.299 --> 00:13:09.750
He bought a macaroni company, and part

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:12.399
of a wine company, hoping that real

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:15.320
profits from real businesses might be

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:17.819
able to pay off his impossible promises.

00:13:18.759 --> 00:13:20.319
They never could.

00:13:21.229 --> 00:13:23.199
And while early publicity

00:13:23.339 --> 00:13:24.599
had been great business for

00:13:24.599 --> 00:13:27.409
Ponzi, he didn't want too much.

00:13:28.419 --> 00:13:31.990
In late July, 1920, the Boston Post

00:13:32.189 --> 00:13:34.570
ran a front-page story about the

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:36.469
miracle worker of School Street.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.799
The following morning brought the

00:13:38.839 --> 00:13:41.209
biggest crowds Ponzi had ever seen,

00:13:41.629 --> 00:13:43.979
pushing fresh hundreds of thousands

00:13:43.979 --> 00:13:45.600
of dollars through his door.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:49.810
The Post's editors also started

00:13:49.889 --> 00:13:51.549
digging, trying to answer the

00:13:51.549 --> 00:13:54.909
question: how, exactly, does

00:13:54.909 --> 00:13:56.699
this man double everyone's money?

00:13:58.089 --> 00:14:00.570
Ponzi had been clear about his methods.

00:14:00.919 --> 00:14:02.229
Reply coupons, he said.

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.439
But something didn't seem to add up.

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:09.509
The Post contacted a man called Clarence

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:11.869
Barron, who was the most respected

00:14:11.889 --> 00:14:13.580
financial journalist in America.

00:14:14.209 --> 00:14:17.260
It asked him to look at the coupon story.

00:14:18.619 --> 00:14:21.130
Barron did some simple sums,

00:14:21.139 --> 00:14:23.130
some back-of-the-envelope maths.

00:14:24.129 --> 00:14:27.160
For Ponzi's scheme to be real, given the

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:31.509
money he had taken in, roughly 160 million

00:14:31.879 --> 00:14:34.419
international reply coupons would need

00:14:34.569 --> 00:14:37.589
to have been bought and moved and cashed.

00:14:38.949 --> 00:14:40.399
Barron checked with

00:14:40.399 --> 00:14:41.690
the postal authorities.

00:14:42.169 --> 00:14:44.349
The total number of coupons

00:14:44.369 --> 00:14:46.439
in circulation, anywhere

00:14:46.459 --> 00:14:49.649
on Earth, was about 27,000.

00:14:50.809 --> 00:14:51.089
Fishy.

00:14:52.279 --> 00:14:54.080
Alarm bells started to ring.

00:14:54.989 --> 00:14:57.440
And there was also the fact that

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:00.270
Ponzi's own money wasn't invested in

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:03.160
the scheme; he kept it in ordinary

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:05.320
banks, earning a comparatively

00:15:05.349 --> 00:15:07.570
pitiful five per cent interest.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:10.920
Here was a man who claimed he could

00:15:10.929 --> 00:15:12.599
double your money in ninety days,

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:15.910
so why wasn't he doing it himself?

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:19.030
If the machine was real, why

00:15:19.059 --> 00:15:20.549
didn't its inventor use it?

00:15:21.949 --> 00:15:23.759
The Post printed all of this

00:15:23.799 --> 00:15:26.289
on its front page, and, well,

00:15:26.609 --> 00:15:28.199
it was not good news for Ponzi.

00:15:29.169 --> 00:15:30.839
The queues outside his office the

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:33.050
following morning weren't there to

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:35.829
put money in, they were from angry

00:15:35.829 --> 00:15:38.489
investors trying to get their money out.

00:15:39.779 --> 00:15:41.930
Ponzi, presumably, knew

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:43.389
this was a possibility.

00:15:43.919 --> 00:15:46.300
He met the crowds personally, looking

00:15:46.579 --> 00:15:49.000
completely calm and unworried.

00:15:49.459 --> 00:15:50.819
He had coffee and doughnuts

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:52.100
brought out to the queue.

00:15:52.629 --> 00:15:55.879
He paid every claim in full, on the spot,

00:15:56.059 --> 00:15:58.669
millions of dollars over a few days,

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:01.789
and he did it with such style that some

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:03.990
people actually changed their minds,

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.539
putting their money back into the scheme.

00:16:07.489 --> 00:16:09.919
It was a magnificent performance.

00:16:10.139 --> 00:16:11.900
But he was finished.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:13.849
The writing was on the wall.

00:16:14.809 --> 00:16:16.870
And the end came from three

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:18.659
directions at the same time.

00:16:19.489 --> 00:16:22.939
First, there was his own publicity man, a

00:16:23.119 --> 00:16:25.760
former journalist named William McMasters.

00:16:26.449 --> 00:16:28.829
He had seen enough from inside the

00:16:28.829 --> 00:16:31.150
operation to understand exactly what was

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:34.289
happening, and he later described Ponzi as

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:37.249
"a financial idiot" who could barely add.

00:16:38.459 --> 00:16:40.969
McMasters went to the Boston Post,

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:43.829
and at the start of August the paper

00:16:43.829 --> 00:16:46.669
declared, on his evidence, that Ponzi

00:16:46.839 --> 00:16:50.329
was hopelessly insolvent, that his debts

00:16:50.489 --> 00:16:52.969
were far bigger than everything he owned.

00:16:54.389 --> 00:16:57.199
Then came the state's bank examiners.

00:16:57.969 --> 00:17:00.059
They had been watching Ponzi's enormous

00:17:00.119 --> 00:17:03.469
overdrafts with alarm, and they moved

00:17:03.479 --> 00:17:05.859
in on Hanover Trust and shut it down.

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:10.189
And then the auditors finished counting.

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:12.929
Ponzi's company's debts were

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:15.120
bigger than everything it owned,

00:17:15.309 --> 00:17:17.390
by around seven million dollars.

00:17:17.979 --> 00:17:20.509
There was no vault of stamps, and

00:17:20.898 --> 00:17:22.859
there were no warehouses of coupons.

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:25.839
In the entire life of a business

00:17:25.929 --> 00:17:28.019
supposedly built on international reply

00:17:28.019 --> 00:17:31.149
coupons, the auditors found that Ponzi had

00:17:31.149 --> 00:17:34.380
bought a mere sixty-one dollars' worth.

00:17:35.509 --> 00:17:37.739
On the 12th of August 1920,

00:17:38.019 --> 00:17:39.620
less than eight months after he

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.579
started, Charles Ponzi surrendered

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:44.209
to the federal authorities.

00:17:45.459 --> 00:17:48.009
The final reckoning was brutal.

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:50.980
Somewhere between fifteen and twenty

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:52.679
million dollars had gone into the

00:17:52.679 --> 00:17:55.470
scheme, taken from tens of thousands

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:57.570
of ordinary people, and worth

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:00.370
hundreds of millions in today's terms.

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:04.000
Investors eventually got back less

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:05.929
than thirty cents for each dollar.

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:08.219
At least five banks

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:09.649
collapsed along with it.

00:18:10.789 --> 00:18:13.149
Ponzi pleaded guilty to federal

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:15.070
fraud charges in November

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:18.009
1920 and got five years.

00:18:18.599 --> 00:18:20.369
The state of Massachusetts then

00:18:20.389 --> 00:18:22.289
pursued him for further charges,

00:18:22.549 --> 00:18:24.999
and eventually added another seven

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:26.739
to nine years to the sentence.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:30.530
The mansion, the luxury car, it all went.

00:18:31.129 --> 00:18:33.289
Ponzi was left with nothing.

00:18:34.599 --> 00:18:37.439
He appealed the charge, and oddly

00:18:37.439 --> 00:18:39.489
enough, was released on bail.

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:42.670
And Ponzi being Ponzi, he

00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:44.339
couldn't resist the urge to get

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:45.909
another scheme up and running.

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:48.729
He fled to Florida where he planned

00:18:48.759 --> 00:18:51.449
to capitalise on a real estate boom.

00:18:52.539 --> 00:18:54.889
Of course, it was another fraud:

00:18:55.539 --> 00:18:57.889
he promised investors an outrageous

00:18:57.889 --> 00:19:01.689
return of 200 per cent in sixty days,

00:19:02.179 --> 00:19:05.089
but the land he sold was swampland.

00:19:05.639 --> 00:19:08.419
Wet, often literally underwater,

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:10.329
and utterly worthless.

00:19:11.349 --> 00:19:13.229
It didn't take long for it to

00:19:13.229 --> 00:19:15.470
fail, and when he was found out,

00:19:15.759 --> 00:19:17.619
he was arrested and convicted.

00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:21.460
Again he appealed the conviction, was

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:25.089
released on bond, and yet again he fled.

00:19:25.939 --> 00:19:27.769
This time, though, it seemed

00:19:27.769 --> 00:19:29.109
like he took it more seriously.

00:19:29.549 --> 00:19:31.569
He shaved his head, grew a

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:33.909
moustache, and signed on as a

00:19:33.931 --> 00:19:36.639
deckhand on a ship bound for Italy.

00:19:37.519 --> 00:19:40.569
And he would have made it had he been

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:42.879
able to keep his true identity a secret.

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:45.100
He could not.

00:19:45.479 --> 00:19:47.339
He couldn't resist telling a shipmate

00:19:47.549 --> 00:19:50.349
who he was, and before the ship took to

00:19:50.349 --> 00:19:52.489
the Atlantic, the police arrested him.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:55.789
This time, there would be no bail.

00:19:56.979 --> 00:19:57.780
He served out his

00:19:57.790 --> 00:19:59.509
sentence in Massachusetts.

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:02.889
And in 1934, he was deported to

00:20:02.929 --> 00:20:06.269
Italy, grey, heavier, and looking

00:20:06.319 --> 00:20:08.099
twenty years older than he was.

00:20:09.199 --> 00:20:12.679
His last years were a long, sad decline.

00:20:13.339 --> 00:20:15.879
A divorce from his long-suffering wife.

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:18.380
A string of failed schemes in Rome.

00:20:18.619 --> 00:20:21.270
A job in Brazil with an Italian airline.

00:20:21.659 --> 00:20:22.904
Work as a translator.

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:25.529
A stroke, which left him partially

00:20:25.529 --> 00:20:28.169
paralysed and blind in one eye.

00:20:29.429 --> 00:20:31.739
Charles Ponzi died in January

00:20:31.739 --> 00:20:34.700
1949, in the charity ward of

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.769
a hospital in Rio de Janeiro.

00:20:37.429 --> 00:20:39.960
He was practically destitute, with his

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.420
estate coming to seventy-five dollars,

00:20:42.699 --> 00:20:44.919
just about enough to pay for his funeral.

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:47.650
Did he regret it?

00:20:48.649 --> 00:20:51.292
If he did, he certainly didn't show it.

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:54.600
In an interview with an American

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:56.659
reporter from his hospital bed

00:20:57.110 --> 00:20:58.840
there was no trace of apology.

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:01.720
Those Bostonians had got their

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:03.089
money's worth, he insisted.

00:21:03.679 --> 00:21:05.799
"Without malice aforethought, I had

00:21:05.809 --> 00:21:07.699
given them the best show that was

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:09.720
ever staged in their territory since

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:11.599
the landing of the Pilgrims. It was

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:13.679
easily worth fifteen million bucks

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:15.219
to watch me put the thing over."

00:21:16.769 --> 00:21:19.100
He came to the United States with two

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:21.299
dollars and fifty cents to his name,

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:23.739
intending to make something of himself.

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:26.750
A hundred years later, people

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:29.109
do remember Charles Ponzi, or at

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:30.979
least they know the word Ponzi.

00:21:31.639 --> 00:21:33.429
It is just spelt with a small

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:35.878
"p" these days, and sits in the

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:38.549
dictionary, filed under fraud.

00:21:40.039 --> 00:21:42.079
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:21:42.079 --> 00:21:44.229
episode on Charles Ponzi, the

00:21:44.230 --> 00:21:46.270
man who gave fraud his name.

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:48.549
And with that comes an end

00:21:48.559 --> 00:21:49.999
to our three-part mini-series

00:21:50.119 --> 00:21:51.729
on famous con artists.

00:21:52.118 --> 00:21:54.372
In case you missed parts one and two,

00:21:54.652 --> 00:21:56.807
those were on Victor Lustig, the man

00:21:56.807 --> 00:21:58.932
who sold the Eiffel Tower, and Gregor

00:21:58.932 --> 00:22:01.692
MacGregor, the man who invented a country.

00:22:02.252 --> 00:22:03.762
And if you want to hear the

00:22:03.762 --> 00:22:05.942
tale of another, real-life Ponzi

00:22:05.942 --> 00:22:09.372
scheme, episode number 167 tells

00:22:09.372 --> 00:22:11.152
the story of Bernie Madoff.

00:22:11.702 --> 00:22:13.592
You've been listening to English Learning

00:22:13.592 --> 00:22:16.152
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:22:16.562 --> 00:22:19.782
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:22:19.802 --> 00:22:21.972
I'll catch you in the next episode.

